Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 15:21:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 39/52] perf(canvas): a render trial decides the GPU layer and
 zoom pictures per browser

Both render experiments (the GPU picture layer, doop:gpu, and zoom
pictures, doop:zoom-pictures) help on some devices and cost on others,
and nobody turns a localStorage switch on. With this, each browser tries
them and keeps what is smoother for it, and reports what it measured
(feat(telemetry)'s route) so the arms can be compared across devices.

Who decides, read once per page load (src/lib/renderTrialClient.ts)
- An explicit key wins and takes its factor out of the trial: doop:gpu =
  off | on | force, doop:zoom-pictures = on | off. Any other value of
  doop:gpu is off, as before.
- doop:render-trial = off switches the trial off: unset experiments are
  off, nothing is measured or sent. force lets the trial put the GPU
  layer on a software GL (for measuring on a machine without a GPU).
- Otherwise the trial assigns each factor independently, a 2x2 factorial
  (arm A neither, B GPU, C both, D zoom pictures only).
- The levels are fixed at the first read, so the Stage, every frame and
  the GPU layer agree for the whole load. gpuMode() and
  zoomPicturesEnabled() now return the trial's decision; before, the zoom
  pictures key was read again at each use.

Eligibility for the GPU layer
Hardware WebGL2 only, the gate Mapbox GL uses: a context with
failIfMajorPerformanceCaveat, and an unmasked renderer that does not name
SwiftShader, llvmpipe, softpipe or "Software". Probed once per browser and
epoch, both contexts released at once. Culling off makes it ineligible
(nothing is culled to draw). If the layer fails to start or loses its
context on a load the trial put it on, that load's frames are taken back
from on and the load switches to off; after 2 failures the factor is
ineligible until the next epoch. A failure under an explicit key is not
counted.

The recorder (GestureRecorder in src/lib/renderTrial.ts)
A subscription of its own in Stage, beside the camera's, so apply() and
its rest timers are unchanged. It notes the time of each viewport change
and runs one requestAnimationFrame loop only while a gesture is on; a
gesture ends 150 ms after the last change, unless the camera changed
since the frame before: a slow frame must not split a gesture (it did at
first, and on the stress page six pinches came out as 14 gestures whose
short pieces, slow frames among them, were dropped). An interval counts when the
camera changed within the two frames before it ends (the frame that drew
the change and the one its raster lands in), so the idle tail and the
camera's rest timers are left out. Intervals while hidden and over 1 s
are ignored; gestures of fewer than 3 changes (the fit on opening, a fly
to a frame) are jumps and not counted. A frame is slow above 1.5x the
display interval, estimated as each gesture's p10 interval, taken at
once when faster and moved 5% toward it when slower, so a gesture full
of slow frames does not teach it that the display is slow (clamped to
240-24 Hz). Per gesture kind (pan, or zoom if the zoom changed at all),
stratum (heavy: at least 30 culled frames in view at its start) and
live (a live document in view) it keeps frames, slow frames, the sum and
a 43-bin histogram (1 ms bins from 4 to 34 ms) for the p95. What is in
view is counted once, at the gesture's start, from the store; the loop
reads no DOM and allocates nothing per frame.

The decision (epsilon-greedy, src/lib/renderTrial.ts)
A factor is judged on every gesture it can change: gpu on each gesture
that starts with a culled frame in view (the layer draws those and
redraws its canvas on every camera frame, so crowded pans alone would let
it win where it costs on a pinch: on a software GL the pinch below went
from 14/360 slow frames to 157/360 with it), zoomPictures on zooms with
a live document in view; other gestures are reported but teach nothing.
State
is one versioned JSON value, doop:render-trial-data, re-read before
each write (two tabs: the last write wins, and both are samples).
- Explore until both levels have 600 judged frames and 3 judged loads.
  A level is drawn at random until one is 2 loads ahead, then the other
  is due (the big stick design, Soares and Wu 1983). Strict alternation
  after a random first load would give the two factors complementary
  levels load after load (A then C, or B then D), and one factor's
  effect would be read as the other's.
- on wins only if its slow-frame share is lower by at least 2 points and
  at least 20% relative; otherwise off. With no slow frame at either
  level off wins and the verdict is closed: no epsilon, so a browser
  where nothing is slow ends exactly where it was before the trial.
- With an open verdict, epsilon = 0.1 of loads take the other level, so a
  driver update or a heavier canvas can turn it.
- Past 20000 frames in a level both levels are halved (loads floored at
  the minimum, so halving never sends a decided factor back to explore).
- A browser that had each level 12 times without the frames to judge
  (it never makes heavy gestures, say) stops exploring: off.
- TRIAL_EPOCH resets every browser's state, probe included. Garbage in
  storage makes this load run untrialed and writes a fresh state;
  storage that cannot be written means no trial.

The report
On hide and on pagehide, otherwise at most every 5 minutes when a
gesture ends; nothing when nothing was measured. sendBeacon with a
string body (text/plain, no preflight), else fetch with keepalive. It
holds the epoch, each factor's level and reason (explore, exploit,
epsilon, override, ineligible, off), the device class (renderer cut to
80 printable characters, hardware flag, DPR, the window rounded to 100
px, hardwareConcurrency, deviceMemory, the display interval, touch, a
frame-count bucket), the rows since the last report and the verdicts.
No ids, names or HTML: the server's closed schema could not take them.

What the recorder costs: its animation-frame loop asks for a frame on
every display interval while a gesture is on, so a wheel that sends an
event every 40 ms gets empty frames in between. A 30-event wheel pan,
recorder off against on, three interleaved rounds: full compositor
updates 30-32 against 30-31 and their time 128-199 ms against 123-206 ms
(unchanged), commits 90 against 122-130 (the empty ones, 46-75 ms
against 50-74 ms in all), main thread 257-414 ms against 278-409 ms. A
trackpad sends an event per frame and gets no extra frames.

Measured (headless Chromium, SwiftShader GL, 1600x1000, a 188-frame
page; the trial's own rows, slow frames / frames, two interleaved rounds,
load 8-20 on 8 cores, so absolute times are loose and the counts are the
point). Pan at fit, about 90 culled frames in view, 3 gestures of 40
wheel events each: GPU off 11/120 and 4/120, GPU force 63/120 and
63/120 (p95 34 ms against 97-109 ms). A software GL moves the work off
the main thread (renderer task 1603/1261 ms against 440/353 ms) and
makes frames slower, which is why the gate asks for hardware. Pinch at
60% over live documents, 6 gestures of 30 frames: zoom pictures off
46/180 and 15/180, on 0/180 and 0/180. The intervals tell the arms
apart. All four arms, a later run of two rounds (load 4-11), slow
frames / frames summed: pan at fit A 0/240, D 0/240, B 61/240, C 56/240;
pinch at 60% A 14/360, D 0/360, B 157/360 (mean 51-55 ms), C 22/360.
Not yet measured on a real GPU or on a phone.

Tests: tests/renderTrial.test.ts (assignment at random and by turn, the
two factors not paired, the margins at
their edges, zero slow, the explore budget, epsilon, the cap, removing a
failed load, eligibility, garbage and epoch in storage, the histogram,
the recorder's counting, tail, a slow frame inside a gesture, jumps, hide, display estimate and rows,
the scene, a report valid for the server); tests/renderTrialBrowser.test.ts
in Chromium (nothing set: assigned and stored, a pan and a zoom become
rows, a hide sends one valid report with nothing of the canvas in it; an
explicit key wins and teaches nothing; the switch off sends nothing).
tests/browser.ts sets doop:render-trial = off for every other browser
test unless it sets the key, so their experiments stay as written.

